{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And against all the congregation it shall break forth.</b> — it shall be [that] wrath [breaks forth]. For it is not fitting to say that their punishment would fall upon Israel, for the Holy One, blessed be He, does not punish one nation for the sin of another nation, and the priests are a people unto themselves. Nor is the intent that HaShem would be wrathful against Israel if the priests let their hair loose and rend their garments; rather, its meaning is that wrath would fall upon the entirety of Israel when they would need to mourn and weep over them. And like it (2 Samuel 2:1): ״ויאור להם בחברון״, whose meaning is ויהי אור, and so too here, יקצוף — 'it shall break forth' — means יהיה קצף — 'there shall be wrath.' And just as we find concerning Moses our master, peace be upon him (Deuteronomy 34:8): ״ויבכו בני ישראל את משה בערבות מואב״, and also concerning Aaron (Numbers 20:29): ״ויבכו את אהרן שלשים יום כל בית ישראל״, so it says here: ״ואחיכם כל בית ישראל יבכו את השרפה״ — for it is well known that among all Israel there were none greater in rank than Nadav and Avihu, who were on a level above all the elders after Moses and Aaron; as it is written (Exodus 24:1): ״ואל משה אמר עלה אל ה' אתה ואהרן נדב ואביהוא ושבעים מזקני ישראל״. And they further added and increased in their weeping over them beyond what they did for Moses and Aaron, because the commandment came to them from Moses our master, peace be upon him, as it says: ״ואחיכם כל בית ישראל יבכו את השרפה״; thus wrote Rabbenu Saadiah, of blessed memory."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:24:35.534126472Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}